    "format-version": "0.1",
    "name": "Part3_TRANSIT_Analysis",
    "readme": "2. TRANSIT Analysis Workflow\n\nPurpose\nThis workflow performs downstream TRANSIT analysis using WIG files generated by the Tn-seq preprocessing workflow.\n\nThe workflow performs two complementary analyses:\n1. TRANSIT Resampling: differential fitness analysis between an experimental condition and a control.\n2. TRANSIT Gumbel: condition-specific gene essentiality analysis performed independently for the control and experimental conditions.\n\n\nINPUTS\n\n1. Control WIG\n   - WIG file representing the control condition.\n   - Example: Untreated_WIG\n   - The file should contain TA-site position and raw read count.\n   - Zero-count TA sites should be retained.\n\n2. Experiment_WIG\n   - WIG file representing the experimental or treated condition.\n   - Example: Treated1_WIG, Van20_WIG, Ery10_WIG\n   - Must be generated using the same reference genome and preprocessing procedure as the Control WIG.\n\n3. TRANSIT_annotation\n   - TRANSIT-compatible prot_table annotation file.\n   - Generated from the reference GFF/GFF3 file using:\n     \"Convert GFF3 to prot_table for TRANSIT\"\n   - The annotation must correspond to the same genome assembly used to generate the WIG files.\n\n\nANALYSIS 1 \u2014 TRANSIT RESAMPLING\n\nPurpose:\nIdentify genes showing differential insertion fitness between the experimental and control conditions.\n\nInput direction:\nExperiment_WIG \u2192 Input .wig files\nControl WIG    \u2192 Control .wig files\nTRANSIT_annotation \u2192 Input annotation\n\nSettings:\n- Operation mode: Batch\n- Number of samples: 10000\n- Output histogram of permutations: No\n- Perform adaptive resampling: No\n- Exclude rows with zero across conditions: No\n- Add pseudocounts at each site: No\n- Perform LOESS correction: No\n- N-terminal exclusion: 0.0\n- C-terminal exclusion: 0.0\n- Normalization: TTR\n\nImportant:\n\"Number of samples = 10000\" refers to the number of resampling/permutation iterations.\nIt does NOT represent the number of biological samples.\n\nWorkflow output:\nResampling - Experiment vs Control\n\n\nANALYSIS 2 \u2014 TRANSIT GUMBEL: CONTROL\n\nPurpose:\nDetermine gene essentiality within the control condition.\n\nInput:\nControl WIG\nTRANSIT_annotation\n\nSettings:\n- Operation mode: Batch\n- How to handle replicates: Sum\n- Number of samples: 10000\n- Number of Burn-in samples: 500\n- Smallest read-count to consider: 1\n- Trimming interval for values: 1\n- N-terminal exclusion: 0.0\n- C-terminal exclusion: 0.0\n- Normalization: TTR\n\nWorkflow output:\nGumbel - Control\n\n\nANALYSIS 3 \u2014 TRANSIT GUMBEL: EXPERIMENT\n\nPurpose:\nDetermine gene essentiality within the experimental condition.\n\nInput:\nExperiment_WIG\nTRANSIT_annotation\n\nSettings:\nSame as Control Gumbel.\n\nWorkflow output:\nGumbel - Experiment\n\n\nINTERPRETATION\n\nTRANSIT Resampling:\nUse this result for the direct statistical comparison between Experiment and Control.\nThe analysis identifies genes with differential insertion fitness between the two conditions.\n\nTRANSIT Gumbel:\nGumbel is NOT a direct Control-versus-Experiment statistical comparison.\nIt determines gene essentiality independently within each condition.\n\nTypical Gumbel calls:\nE  = Essential\nEB = Essential according to the binomial component/classification\nNE = Non-essential\nU  = Uncertain\nS  = Too small / insufficient TA-site information\n\nFor example:\nControl NE \u2192 Experiment E\nmay indicate candidate condition-specific essentiality.\n\nHowever, a change in Gumbel classification is not itself a statistical test of the difference between conditions.\nUse TRANSIT Resampling for differential-fitness testing and Gumbel as complementary condition-specific essentiality information.\n\n\nIMPORTANT NOTES\n\n- Control and Experiment WIG files must be generated against the same reference genome.\n- TRANSIT_annotation must correspond to that same genome assembly.\n- Use raw TA-site read counts as WIG input. Do not manually normalize the WIG files before TRANSIT.\n- Keep zero-count TA sites in the WIG files.\n- In Resampling, always check the analysis direction:\n      Input .wig = Experiment\n      Control .wig = Control\n- TTR normalization is performed within TRANSIT.\n- Gumbel should be run independently for each condition.\n- For multiple experimental conditions, run separate Experiment-versus-Control comparisons.\n  Example:\n      Treated1 vs Untreated\n      Treated2 vs Untreated\n- For biological replicates, generate one WIG per replicate. Do not manually merge replicate WIG files before analysis. Use Operation mode = Replicates in TRANSIT.\n\nWORKFLOW STRUCTURE\n\nControl WIG \u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2510\n                     \u251c\u2500\u2500 TRANSIT Resampling\nExperiment_WIG \u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2518\n                     \u2193\n            Experiment vs Control\n\nControl WIG \u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2192 TRANSIT Gumbel\n                          \u2193\n                     Control essentiality\n\nExperiment_WIG \u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2192 TRANSIT Gumbel\n                          \u2193\n                   Experiment essentiality\n\nTRANSIT_annotation \u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2500\u2192 used by all three analyses",
    "report": {
